# Pharmaceutical-Grade Extraction Blueprint for Chefaa Medications Batch 5 (Products 801–1000)

## Executive Summary: Scope, Objectives, and Outcomes

This blueprint specifies the extraction approach, standards, and clinical schema for the pharmaceutical-grade curation of Chefaa’s Medications Batch 5 covering products 801–1000. The effort centers on detailed clinical fields that enable clinical decision support, editorial consistency, and downstream analytics for Egyptian market pharmaceutical data.

The batch scope aligns with catalog pages 41–50 within Chefaa’s medications category, with an expected volume of ~200 products (approximately 20 per page). We anchor the range and the source catalog context explicitly to ensure traceability and auditability[^1][^2].

Objectives are threefold. First, to assemble structured, clinically credible profiles from product detail pages and Chefaa blog explainers: description, active ingredient(s) and strengths, mechanism of action (when available), indications, dosing and administration, safety (warnings, side effects, contraindications), pregnancy and breastfeeding considerations, storage, and regulatory fields (manufacturer, brand, and platform-level identifiers when present). Second, to enforce a uniform schema and coding dictionary across all items. Third, to establish quality controls and consistency checks that guarantee clinical coherence and internal cross-product comparability.

Status as of this blueprint: coverage to date includes 13 products with extracted detail and numerous catalog listings awaiting detailed page extraction. Several products have robust clinical content suitable for immediate modeling—Muco SR (ambroxol), Mucobrave (acetylcysteine), Movxir (methocarbamol + diclofenac), Multi Relax (cyclobenzaprine), Mucosol (carbocisteine), Orly (orlistat), Gaptin (gabapentin), and Nanazoxid pediatric syrup (nitazoxanide)—while others remain partially specified (e.g., Mycosta, Nasostop variants) and require completion. Notable information gaps include missing mechanisms, unspecified active ingredients for some catalog items, absent storage conditions for many entries, and sporadic absence of contraindications and side-effect classifications.

Next steps: complete targeted extraction for remaining catalog items, fill gaps with Chefaa blog explainers and product pages, normalize all units and terminology, and finalize the consolidated dataset for the 200 products in this batch, with a completeness and quality audit prior to sign-off.

## Source Catalog Mapping and Provenance

Batch 5 maps to Chefaa medications catalog pages 41–50. This range anchors the product universe for products 801–1000 and provides consistent provenance to the category-level view[^1]. The specific page mapping ensures reproducibility and controlled scope[^2].

Product-page discovery relies on catalog listing links and internal site search, supported by Chefaa blog explainers for mechanism, dosing, and safety when product pages are sparse. This provenance model prioritizes primary product pages for strength, pack, pricing, dosing, and administration; blog explainers are used to enrich mechanism and safety when the item page is limited.

Language handling is integral to extraction fidelity. Arabic product and brand names are normalized to canonical forms with transliteration preserved. Units are standardized to mg, ml, and percent concentrations, and dosage forms mapped to controlled vocabulary (tablet, capsule, syrup, effervescent sachet, nasal drops, cream, gel, ophthalmic solution).

Pricing and policies are captured verbatim when present on item pages (e.g., “delivery within 30–60 minutes,” “exchange or return allowed”), with explicit attribution to the product page context. Regulatory fields include manufacturer and brand when available; platform-level tax registration numbers are recorded as encountered but not treated as product-specific regulatory identifiers.

## Clinical Data Schema and Extraction Specification

A single, unambiguous schema enables comparable clinical profiles across diverse dosage forms and therapeutic classes. The schema is designed to be both exhaustive and adaptable to future batches.

Core fields:
- Canonical identifiers: product ID (Chefaa or composite key), product name (Arabic and English), brand, manufacturer.
- Product form and route: dosage form, route (controlled vocabulary), pack size, strength(s).
- Active ingredients: array of ingredient objects (name, strength, unit, pharmacological class).
- Mechanism of action (MoA): concise, clinically oriented description.
- Indications: array of canonical indication phrases (e.g., “cough with phlegm,” “muscle spasm”).
- Dosing and administration: adult and pediatric dosing, frequency, maximum daily dose, duration, route-specific instructions.
- Safety: warnings, side-effect classification (common, less common, rare, serious), contraindications.
- Pregnancy and breastfeeding: normalized guidance (not recommended, contraindicated, consult physician, insufficient data).
- Storage: temperature, post-opening use-by, keep out of reach of children.
- Regulatory and policy: manufacturer, brand, tax/registration number (platform-level where present), return/delivery policy, prescription status.
- Pricing: price (EGP), currency, unit/pack.

Coding dictionaries enforce consistency:

- Dosage forms: tablet, film-coated tablet, capsule, syrup, effervescent sachet, nasal drops, ophthalmic solution/eye drops, cream, gel, massage cream.
- Routes: oral, topical, nasal, ophthalmic.
- Therapeutic classes: mucolytic, expectorant, skeletal muscle relaxant, NSAID, decongestant (alpha-agonist), anti-parasitic (nitroimidazole), PDE5 inhibitor, anticonvulsant/antiepileptic, dietary supplement (osteoarthritic joint support).
- Units: mg, ml, percent (for nasal/ophthalmic preparations).

Classification of adverse events:
- Common: ≥1% to <10%.
- Less common: ≥0.1% to <1%.
- Rare: <0.1%.
- Serious: events requiring immediate medical attention or hospitalization.

These thresholds align with standard clinical reporting conventions and facilitate safety comparisons across products.

