## Risk, Safety, and Special Population Guidance

Special populations—pediatrics, pregnancy, breastfeeding—require explicit handling.

Pediatric exclusions and dosing caps are critical. Nasostop pediatric nasal drops prohibit use under two years, require medical supervision under six years, limit dosing to ≤3 times per day, and restrict continuous use to ≤3 days[^10]. Mucosol syrup advises against use under two years and provides age-banded pediatric dosing[^8]. Nanazoxid pediatric syrup sets lower age limits (≥1 year) and specifies age-appropriate doses for three days[^7].

Pregnancy contraindications are explicit for orlistat (contraindicated) and for acetylcysteine (contraindicated in pregnancy; physician consultation required). Cyclobenzaprine carries pregnancy category B; use requires physician assessment; breastfeeding may be compatible with monitoring[^4][^6][^13]. Fixed-dose combinations with NSAIDs list late pregnancy contraindications and breastfeeding non-recommendation[^5]. Gabapentin is contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation per the item page[^14].

Drug interaction signals are prominent in this batch. Cyclobenzaprine contraindication with MAOIs and caution with QT-prolonging drugs necessitate red-flag symptoms and timing guidance[^6]. Orlistat requires spacing with cyclosporine and levothyroxine and monitoring for warfarin anticoagulation; diarrhea may reduce oral contraceptive efficacy, requiring backup contraception[^13]. Fixed-dose muscle/NSAID combinations require careful co-administered anticoagulant, CNS depressant, lithium, and methotrexate counseling[^5]. Acetaminophen and ibuprofen have known timing and interaction profiles, though not specifically detailed in the extracted pages; class-level cautions apply.

Storage guidance must be captured when present. Pediatric nasal drops require use within one month after opening; unopened validity follows the expiry date[^10]. Many items lack storage details and must be flagged and queued for manual verification.

