## QA Procedures and Completion Controls

Quality assurance is the backbone of clinical data integrity.

Normalization steps include name and brand canonicalization with transliteration, standardized dosage form and route mapping, and unit standardization to mg/ml/percent. Doses are captured with explicit age groups, frequency, maximum daily doses, and duration limits. Side effects are classified per the thresholds defined in Table 3.

Completeness checks enforce mandatory fields: dosage form, route, active ingredient(s) and strength(s), core indications, basic adult dosing, price, pack size, and brand/manufacturer. Mechanism, pediatric dosing, contraindications, side-effect classification, and storage conditions are captured when available; if absent, flagged as “not specified” and queued for targeted follow-up.

Provenance requires that each field carry a source indicator: “product page,” “blog explainer,” or “external reference (with citation ID).” External references are reserved for well-established class-level details and not used for product-specific regulatory assertions.

Cross-batch consistency is maintained by the controlled vocabularies and thresholds defined above. The internal consistency checks include:
- Route-form alignment (e.g., nasal drops must have nasal route).
- Dose units alignment with dosage form (e.g., mg for tablets/capsules; ml for syrups; percent for nasal/ophthalmic).
- Dosing frequency reasonableness against class patterns.
- Safety classification against thresholds for comparability.

## Output Packaging and Delivery Plan

The final dataset for Batch 5 is delivered as a single consolidated JSON file containing an array of product objects conforming to the schema specified above. Each product object includes structured fields for identifiers, form/route, active ingredients, mechanism (when available), indications, dosing and administration, safety (warnings, side effects, contraindications), pregnancy/breastfeeding guidance, storage, regulatory and policy details, pricing, and provenance flags.

Validation flags accompany items missing any mandatory fields or critical safety elements (e.g., contraindications, side effects, storage). Fields that are “not specified” must be resolved via targeted re-crawls or manual clinical review, with a defined follow-up queue.

## Information Gaps and Targeted Remediation Plan

Batch 5 has several recurring gaps and targeted remedies:

- Missing product detail pages: Some catalog items lack comprehensive clinical fields. Action: prioritize internal site search and blog explainers; if absent, mark fields “not specified” and assign to manual clinical review.
- Mechanism details: Many items lack explicit MoA. Action: supplement from Chefaa blog explainers; avoid external augmentation except for established class-level facts.
- Active ingredient specificity: Select items (e.g., Mycosta) do not list active ingredients. Action: targeted follow-up extraction; if unresolved, flag for manual curation.
- Storage conditions: Frequently absent. Action: retain “not specified” and queue for verification; add standard poisoning prevention when appropriate.
- Interaction profiles: Sparse in some products. Action: enrich from class-level data (e.g., NSAIDs, PDE5 inhibitors, anticonvulsants) with caution and explicit attribution.
- Pediatric and pregnancy/breastfeeding guidance: Partial coverage. Action: default to conservative phrasing (“consult physician”) when class-level safety is insufficient.
- Regulatory details: Many entries show platform-level tax IDs rather than product-specific regulatory data. Action: capture as is; do not infer product-level regulatory status.

